# Hybrid stepper motor with a current-controlled microstepping driver.
part stepper_motor "Stepper motor with microstepping driver"
category Actuators
summary "A stepper: the driver sets the rotor's magnetic target angle; the rotor follows like a stiff spring, or slips a step if overloaded."
explain "A hybrid stepper has a toothed rotor (50 teeth for 200 steps/rev). The driver pushes set currents through two phases, which pulls the nearest rotor tooth toward a commanded electrical angle. With the driver in current mode, torque depends on the angle error: τ = −T_hold·sin(N·(θ − θ_target)), N = steps/4. Near the target that is a stiff spring (k = T_hold·N); beyond a quarter electrical cycle the torque falls and reverses, so a load above holding torque, or a target that moves too fast for the rotor to follow, makes it slip whole steps: open-loop position is lost. Detent torque from the magnets cogs the rotor at every step even without current."
tradeoffs "Precise, cheap positioning with no encoder and full torque at standstill (3D printers, CNC). But it is open loop: missed steps go unnoticed, torque falls with speed, and it draws full current even while holding still (hot and inefficient). A servo (motor + encoder + controller) is smoother, faster and efficient, at more cost and tuning."
limits "Ideal current-mode driver (no torque drop with speed from back-EMF or supply limits), sinusoidal torque-angle curve, lumped damping for iron loss and driver current-loop effects; no resonance-band modeling beyond what inertia and damping give."
active
pairs part.angle_setpoint rotational.inertia part.timing_belt bridge.lead_screw rotational.ground sensor.encoder part.hard_stop_rotary
port shaft rotational
port case rotational
input target Angle
param holding_torque N·m ~ 0.45 "Holding torque at rated current (NEMA 17: 0.3–0.6 N·m)"
param steps_per_rev 1 = 200 "Full steps per revolution (200 = 1.8°)"
param detent_torque N·m = 0.02 "Magnetic cogging torque with no current"
param damping N·m·s/rad = 0.002 "Iron loss and driver damping"
let rel = shaft.phi - case.phi
let poles = steps_per_rev/4
let torque = holding_torque*sin(poles*(rel - target)) + detent_torque*sin(4*poles*rel) + damping*(shaft.w - case.w)
flow shaft = torque
flow case = -torque
derive "step angle" = 6.283185307/steps_per_rev rad
derive "stiffness near the target" = holding_torque*steps_per_rev/4 N·m/rad
